sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: stage 3 of the command development pipeline. The author proves the command in the development environment it already works in (task VM with its nested fleet). The nested VMs hold their own checkout, with no share from the outer VM, so Incus memory snapshots work. Each run restores the task's base snapshot, syncs the author's code once and takes base again. Each batch restores base once; its cases use their own resources and need no undo. A failed case triggers a restore before the next. Cases come from the command's cases file; each names its batch from swamp/proof/batches.yaml, which sets the order. One shared runner; the same cases and runner run in CI (stage 4, proof:run on a fresh VM).
  %% status: draft
  %% command: development pipeline stage 3 · proof
  %% kind: pipeline.3-proof
  autonumber
  %% lane AG icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane VM icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  %% lane NT icon=server pill=fleet tone=amber
  participant AG as Author<br/>agent or Nick
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>authorizes only
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>owns flow · state
  participant VM as Task VM<br/>outer VM
  participant NT as Nested fleet<br/>Gateway · 2 Nodes
  AG->>GW: Prove the command<br/>orbit proof COMMAND
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - runs the command's cases on the author's own development VM; no new VM
  %%> - the cases file (`swamp/proof/FAMILY/COMMAND.cases.yaml`) names the batch of each case; `swamp/proof/batches.yaml` defines the shared batches and their order; nothing is picked at run time
  %%> - one command per run; in CI, `proof:run` proves each affected command separately, on its own proof VM
  %%> Not built:
  %%> - the command `orbit proof COMMAND`; today `proof:run` is the only way in from the Gateway. Its parent workflow `operation-proof-run` also runs on an existing task VM: with `intent.existing` it neither provisions nor destroys a VM, and the author's own VM works that way
  %%> - built: the cases file format, the runner and the Swamp workflows `proof-fleet-verify`, `proof-fleet-verify-batch` and `proof-fleet-verify-case`, which run the batches and the cases below
  Note over GW: Authorize<br/>mint tokens
  GW->>SW: Start the proof<br/>cases file · this VM
  SW->>NT: Restore the base snapshot<br/>memory included · about 16 s
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - restores the task's Incus stateful snapshot `base` (stage 2), all three VMs in parallel
  %%> - sets each guest clock from the outer VM as soon as that VM's agent answers; a restore puts the clocks back to snapshot time
  %%> - ready when the Gateway answers, every Node agent sends a fresh heartbeat (every 5 s) and Doctor is healthy
  %%> - every restore in this chart has a 45 s limit: a restore that fails or is not healthy in time stops the run, with no retry; the cases that did not run become `unrun` with cause `infrastructure`, and the verdicts of cases that ran stay
  %%> Time, measured on tvm-554:
  %%> - stock Incus restore and agent wait, no tuning: restore about 9 s, healthy about 16 s
  SW->>VM: Sync the author's code<br/>same sync as orbit-nested
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - rsync of the outer checkout into the nested Gateway and app-dev, only what changed since `base`
  %%> - leaves out `vendor`, `node_modules`, `.git`, `.env`, `storage` and `bootstrap/cache`
  %%> - `composer install` or `bun install` only when a lock file changed, from the cache in the VM; then migrations and a Swamp reload
  %%> Time, measured on tvm-554:
  %%> - rsync of both nested checkouts in parallel: 0.2 s with nothing changed, 2.1 s for 7,600 files
  %%> - apply in the nested Gateway (package discovery, migrations, Swamp repository copy, restart of the two proof services): about 1 s
  %%> - `composer install` takes about 10 s even with an unchanged lock file (its scripts regenerate the OpenAPI and MCP files), so it runs only on a lock change
  %%> Not built:
  %%> - the two proof services still wait up to 60 s for the old code share at start (`tools/swamp-proof-guest/boot.py`); that wait goes with the share
  opt playbooks or pins changed
    SW->>VM: Converge once<br/>outer VM · nested Nodes
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - image playbooks on the outer VM; role playbooks and pins on the nested Nodes, through the nested Orbit's `node:converge`
    %%> - runs after the sync, so the nested Orbit has the new playbooks
    %%> Why:
    %%> - a tool update lives in code; this puts it in place once, and the snapshot taken next keeps it
    %%> - the base images are not touched; only the merge and the seed refresh change them
  end
  opt the tokens in base are older than 20 hours
    SW->>NT: Re-mint Swamp tokens<br/>in the nested Gateway
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the nested Gateway mints new serve and worker tokens for its own Swamp, with the same step as the fleet build
    %%> Why:
    %%> - the tokens a build mints last 24 hours and its certificates 2 days; `base` inherits them, so a task older than a day would otherwise prove with an expired nested Swamp
    %%> - these tokens belong to the nested Orbit; the real Gateway mints nothing for them
  end
  opt the sync, the converge or the re-mint changed something
    SW->>NT: Take base again<br/>parallel · about 19 s
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - skipped when rsync moved and deleted nothing, no lock file changed, nothing converged and no token was re-minted: the fleet is then still exactly `base`, as after a rerun without edits
    %%> - `incus snapshot create --stateful --reuse base` on the three nested VMs at once; `--reuse` replaces the old `base`
    %%> - the VMs keep running; their clocks lose about 1 s while memory is saved, which the normal time sync corrects
    %%> - `base` holds the proof Swamp Club key, on the nested Gateway's disk and in its Swamp serve's memory; accepted because `base` belongs to one task, never leaves its task VM and is destroyed with it; ready copies stay key-free
    %%> Time, measured on tvm-554:
    %%> - about 19 s; the Gateway's 660 MB memory state sets it (app-dev 12 s, app-prod 7 s)
    %%> Why:
    %%> - every batch starts from it without another sync; the next proof run only syncs what changed since this one
    %%> - the task VM keeps its own state until the task merges; changes flow back only as code, through the release and the seed refresh
  end
  loop each batch, in the order of swamp/proof/batches.yaml
    SW->>NT: Restore base<br/>about 16 s
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - one restore per batch; skipped when the fleet is still at `base`, as for the first batch
    %%> - 45 s limit, as for every restore; a failed restore stops the run
      opt the batch declares a start state
      SW->>NT: Set up the batch<br/>its declared start state
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - applies what the batch declares, for example dual-stack networking
    end
    loop each case in the batch, in order
      SW->>NT: Run the case<br/>setup · act · expect
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - the shared runner executes the case's spec steps: setup (for example a listener or a test deny), the real CLI call, then the checks the case lists (traffic, UFW, Gateway record, Doctor, unchanged state)
      %%> - each case names the chart path it covers (main, opt, alt); every optional flag has its own case
      %%> - no undo and no cleanup: each case uses its own resources (free ports, its own listeners, a new Instance), so cases in a batch do not touch each other
      %%> - a case that changes shared state (UFW off, injected failures, a held lock, a full-list check) sits in its own batch
      opt the case failed
        SW->>NT: Restore base<br/>then the batch start state
        %%> Why:
        %%> - a failed case can leave anything behind; the next case starts clean
      end
    end
  end
  SW->>NT: Restore base<br/>fleet clean on the author's code
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the author keeps developing on a clean fleet that already runs the latest code; nothing switches back
  %%> Not built:
  %%> - `proof-fleet-verify` does not run this restore; it belongs to the author's own command
  Note over SW: Join the verdicts<br/>report step
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - each case ended with one JSON line of at most 1 KB (Swamp cuts step output at 4,096 bytes): id, verdict, cause, seconds and the failed checks
  %%> - verdicts: `passed`; `failed` with cause `assertion` or `infrastructure`; `unrun` with cause `precondition` (setup failed, the command never ran) or `infrastructure` (a failed restore stopped the run first)
  %%> - the full receipt (every step, command output, firewall state, timings) stays in the task VM's evidence directory
  alt every case passes
    SW-->>GW: Verdict per case<br/>PASS
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Gateway records the verdicts once, from the run's terminal event
    GW-->>AG: PASS per case<br/>timings
  else a case failed or did not run
    SW--xGW: Verdict per case<br/>FAIL with output
    GW--xAG: Failed check per case<br/>no secrets · exit nonzero
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the line names the failed check; the receipt in this task VM has the rest
    %%> - in CI (stage 4) the proof VM is destroyed, so only the verdict lines stay; the author reruns `orbit proof` in their own task VM for the full receipt
  end
  opt a new case is added
    Note over SW,NT: Prove the case can fail<br/>on an isolated checkout
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - removes the mutation on an isolated checkout and confirms the functional assertion fails
    %%> - never mutates a live installation
    %%> Not built:
    %%> - exercising the CI gate with a known failing case (stage 4)
  end
